    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for the USMLE and I'm a bit stuck on the RAAS pathway. Can you explain the mechanism of action for ACE inhibitors, like lisinopril? Specifically, how do they interact with the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to actually lower blood pressure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lisinopril, an ACE inhibitor, works by blocking the Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE). This enzyme is crucial because it converts Angiotensin I into Angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor. By inhibiting this conversion, ACE inhibitors prevent the formation of Angiotensin II, which in turn leads to vasodilation and reduced blood pressure. Furthermore, this reduction in Angiotensin II also decreases aldosterone secretion, leading to less sodium and water retention.",
      "source": "target",
