    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, the cough part is interesting. So if the increased bradykinin is what causes the side effect, is there a way to predict which patients will get the cough and which won't? Also, if we're talking about blocking enzymes and changing peptide levels, does it ever get complicated with how these molecules 'interact' or 'compete' for space at the receptor sites? I'm just trying to visualize how the actual inhibition looks at the molecular level.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Predicting which patients will develop the cough is difficult, as it's not a perfectly predictable trait, but some individuals might be more sensitive to the bradykinin accumulation. In the realm of molecular interaction, the inhibition isn't usually a direct competition for the *same* receptor site in the way a competitive antagonist would block a ligand. Instead, ACE inhibitors are competitive inhibitors of the *enzyme* itself. They bind to the active site of the ACE enzyme, effectively blocking the substrate—whether that substrate is Angiotensin I or bradykinin—from being processed. This is a chemical blockade at the enzyme level, not a receptor-level competition.",
      "source": "target",
